# PowerShell shell (Windows)

The agent shell on this setup is PowerShell. Do not use bash-only constructs in it.

## Forbidden in PowerShell

- Heredoc: `$(cat <<'EOF' ... EOF)`
- Unquoted git upstream: `@{u}` (PowerShell parses `@{...}` as a hashtable). Write `'@{u}'`.
- `export FOO=bar`, bash arrays, `&&` chains that assume bash semantics for `$?`.

## Use instead

- Multi-line text for a commit: a here-string.

```powershell
git commit -m @"
type(scope): subject

body
"@
```

  `git commit -F -` with a here-string does not work: PowerShell passes the text as an argument and git reads it as a pathspec. Use `-m` as above, or commit from Bash with a heredoc.
- Long text for `gh`: write a temporary UTF-8 file and pass `--body-file`, then delete it (`.cursor/rules/scratch.mdc`).
- Do not put `Remove-Item` in the same command as a path under `C:\Program Files ...`: the agent's PowerShell tool reads `C:\Program` as the item to remove and refuses the whole command. Remove files in a separate call, or from Bash.
- A helper script (Python, a patch): write it to a file in the scratchpad first, then run it. An edit whose text holds backslashes (a C macro's line ends, `\0` in a string, a regular expression) is always made this way, never through a heredoc or a command argument: in M6a a heredoc passed to Python lost the `\` of a C macro and broke the match of a `\0` string (PR #138). Do not build it from a here-string piped to `Set-Content` in the same command that removes files.
- Native commands set `$LASTEXITCODE`. Check it before the next step: `dotnet test ...; if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { exit $LASTEXITCODE }`.
- Variable names are not case-sensitive. A local `$low` is the parameter `$Low`. Give locals names that differ from every parameter in more than case. In F1 a loop's `$version` overwrote the script's `-Version`, and the SBOM named the last npm package's version as ElasticLM's (PR #154).
- Do not name a variable after an automatic variable (`$host`, `$input`, `$args`, `$matches`, `$error`): `$host` is read-only, and the script stops where it is assigned.
- A PowerShell script that a GitHub Actions step runs ends with `exit 0` after its last check. The step takes the last native command's `$LASTEXITCODE` as its result: in F1 `codesign -dv` of an unsigned `osx-x64` binary returned 1 after every check had passed, and the job failed (PR #154).
- Splatting one string (`& $app @args` where `$args` is `'--text'`) passes its characters one by one. Build arguments as an array: `[string[]]$arguments = @(...)`.
- Run the admin UI's Node.js tools (`corepack pnpm install`, `vitest`, `tsc`) from PowerShell with the Node.js of `web/admin/.node-version` first in `PATH`. In Git Bash, `$LOCALAPPDATA` is a Windows path whose `C:` breaks `PATH`, another Node.js runs, and pnpm installs a `node_modules` without the platform's native packages; in M5d2 Vitest then stopped with "Cannot find native binding".
- Run `./build.ps1` in PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`, the agent's PowerShell tool), not Windows PowerShell 5.1 (`powershell.exe`).
- A run that reads an environment variable is run from Bash, with `VAR=value cmd` and `env -u VAR cmd`.
- Quote paths with spaces. Prefer repository-relative paths, except for .NET methods: `[IO.File]::ReadAllText` and the like read a relative path from the process's working directory, not from `Set-Location`. Pass them an absolute path.
- In Bash, `rm -rf "$DIR"/*` is refused by the agent's safety check, because an empty `$DIR` would make it `/*`. Write `"${DIR:?}"/*` or a literal path.

## Writing files

Write files with the editor tools, not with shell redirection. A Python script sent through the Bash tool loses the backslashes of `\f`, `\r`, `\t`, and `\v` in its string literals. After an edit by a script, count the control bytes in the files it wrote with `tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037' < <file> | wc -c`; the count is 0.

A Python script in a Bash heredoc that inserts C# text containing `\n` writes a real line break into the C# string, which does not compile. It happened three times in M2 (PR #12). An edit whose new text holds a C# escape (`\n`, `\t`, `\\`) is made with the editor tool, not with a heredoc script.

A script that makes many edits is itself written with the editor tool into the scratchpad and then run; it is not sent as a heredoc. In M3b, heredoc scripts whose text held quotes and apostrophes broke the Bash parser four times ("unexpected EOF while looking for matching `'`"), and one wrote a raw line break into a Python string (PR #22). It happened again in M5b: an apostrophe in `administrator's` ended the quoting of a Python one-liner, and a `\"` sent through Bash lost its backslash and broke a C# string (PR #114). And in M5c: inside a double-quoted Bash command, `$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)` ran as a shell command and `\a` became a control byte in a csproj (PR #118). Text with `$(`, `\`, or quotes goes into a script file first, always. The Windows Python does not read Bash paths such as `/c/Users/...`: pass `"$(cygpath -w <path>)"`.

The console of this setup uses code page 932. `python -I` ignores `PYTHONIOENCODING`, so a `print` of a character outside cp932 (an en dash, an emoji) stops with `UnicodeEncodeError`. A script whose output may hold such characters writes it to a file opened with `encoding='utf-8'` and reads that file with the editor tool. It stopped twice in M5b while reading an earlier review from a session transcript (PR #111's retrospective).

A command that reads standard input (`cat`, `cat > /dev/null`, `read`) does not go before a heredoc in the same Bash call: it waits for input that never comes, and the call runs until the tool's time limit. In M3c a stray `cat > /dev/null;` before a heredoc script stopped a call for two minutes (PR #27).

In a GitHub Actions step with `shell: pwsh`, the exit code of the last native command becomes the step's result. A step that expects a non-zero exit code (a smoke test of a refusal) ends with `exit 0` after its own check; in M3b the `aot` job failed on all three runners for this alone (PR #22).

Before `git worktree remove` on Windows, run `dotnet build-server shutdown`. A build server that still holds files in the worktree makes the removal stop with "Permission denied" after git has dropped the worktree, and the folder stays behind (after M3d). The same happens when the shell's current directory is inside the worktree: move out of it first (after PR #39). It happened again after PR #175, where the shutdown came after the removal.

A process that does not end by itself (a dev server, a watcher) is started as a background run of the agent's tool and stopped through that run, not started with `&` inside a Bash call. A `&` process outlives the call and holds its files. In W0 a Vite dev server started that way kept listening on `localhost` (IPv6) while a check on `127.0.0.1` saw nothing, and a later removal of `node_modules` failed with `EPERM` until the process was found and stopped (PR #95).

A helper function in a PowerShell script does not take a name that a built-in alias already has. One-letter names are the trap: `r` is `Invoke-History`, `h` is `Get-History`. In PR #179 a script defined `R` for its replacements, every call ran `Invoke-History` and failed, and the script wrote the file back unchanged. Give helpers a verb-noun name (`Set-Text`).

`Copy-Item` keeps the file's last write time, so an incremental build does not see a file put back from a copy. After copying a source file into place, set its time to now (`(Get-Item <file>).LastWriteTime = Get-Date`). In PR #175 a test file put back from a copy kept an older time than the build, MSBuild ran the old test, and the new test looked as if it failed under a forced race.
